# Line endings. Every text file is stored LF in the repository and checked out LF on
# every platform, overriding Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true`.
#
# This is not a style preference. The library inputs are read as bytes by the in-library
# suites -- krlib's `test_the_csvs_are_utf8_without_a_bom` asserts the CSVs carry no BOM
# and no CRLF, because a BOM lands in the first column name and the encoding is
# load-bearing where the provenance columns are Korean. Without the rule below, a Windows
# checkout rewrites every input CSV to CRLF and that test fails on Windows alone, against
# files that are LF in the repository.
#
# `text=auto` leaves the 250 binary files -- the workbooks, pickles and images -- alone,
# because Git detects them rather than taking the rule on trust. lifelib-products carries
# the same pair of rules, for the same test.
* text=auto eol=lf

# `cmd.exe` is the one consumer that wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
